WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.619
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.619 --> 00:00:10.550
&gt;&gt; What does it take to change the world,
a breakthrough idea?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.560
An unparalleled background
in technology and execution?

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.030
Rich, wealthy connections
to Stanford investors?

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.930
Over my ten years of experience in
consulting, in startup financing and

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:31.560
high tech in Silicone Valley,
I've become convinced that we

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.870
are profoundly mistaken in our
conventional wisdom in what it

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:42.110
takes to make breakthrough technology
succeed and to truly change the world.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.410
It took me a moment of meeting the Google
ex co-founder face-to-face to realize

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:54.550
that the techniques that astrophysicists
and laser scientists are using

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:00.810
in Silicon Valley's most innovative
laboratories are simple, repeatable steps

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:07.850
that anyone with access to basic supplies
can start doing at home right away.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:14.300
And even more excitingly, if we were
to really realize this as a society,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.150
our communities, companies and
classrooms would never be the same.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.887
My journey toward learning more
about innovation began when I was

00:01:25.887 --> 00:01:28.409
a young girl in Topeka, Kansas.

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:30.498
When I was young, my greatest hope for

00:01:30.498 --> 00:01:34.886
the future of technology would be that
we would invent a better remote control.

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:37.544
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And my greatest hope for

00:01:37.544 --> 00:01:41.554
government was that we'd find
a way to keep the streets swept,

00:01:41.554 --> 00:01:46.639
particularly in the snow while minimizing
taxes for my dad's small business.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:51.505
Growing up in Kansas, I never imagined
that a non-engineer could work for

00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:53.061
a company like Google.

00:01:53.061 --> 00:01:55.589
But in 2010, I found out.

00:01:55.589 --> 00:01:57.490
In fact, they could.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.200
And after a year of getting my ducks
in a row, I applied for Google and

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.639
I landed my first job there.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.930
Very quickly into my time at Google,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:11.020
I got the opportunity to help build tech
support teams, to help make Gmail and

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.920
other Google apps usable by businesses
large and small around the world.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.776
We started off trying to train people from
many cultures how to answer a simple tech

00:02:21.776 --> 00:02:26.550
question on the phone and resolve it, so
the customer could move on with their day.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.073
But over time we started to realize
that this awesome staff we built

00:02:31.073 --> 00:02:33.590
weren't just tech support agents.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:38.410
In fact, they were very skillful, very
creative humans who could do a lot more

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:44.080
for the humans on the other side of
the phone than we had originally thought.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.240
At first, we started small.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.206
Helping new customers go from fixing an
early problem to anticipating what might

00:02:51.206 --> 00:02:52.089
happen next.

00:02:52.089 --> 00:02:56.361
Then if someone called with one question,
we try and help figure out what was

00:02:56.361 --> 00:03:00.183
actually going on that they were
trying to use technology to solve.

00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:04.743
And then finally, we realized that if
we were really effective at helping our

00:03:04.743 --> 00:03:06.216
customers on the phone,

00:03:06.216 --> 00:03:10.076
we could help them bring technology
to bear on their workforce and

00:03:10.076 --> 00:03:15.390
their company to power present and
future goals that they found important.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.990
The most amazing learning for me in this
process was actually that innovation

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:24.080
doesn't have to happen only for
teams that are developing software.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:31.140
In fact, when you can teach people to
innovate whose work involves other people,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.140
amazing things can happen and
things can transform

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.390
with the smallest intervention
is one interaction on the phone.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:45.481
After Alexis Google, it wasn't long
before I found myself in a very strange

00:03:45.481 --> 00:03:51.360
classroom in an innovation mansion on the
border of Alamo Square in San Francisco.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.357
I stepped through the beautiful entryway,

00:03:54.357 --> 00:03:59.865
looked at the grand staircase coming down
from above and followed red rope lights

00:03:59.865 --> 00:04:05.145
into a mysterious basement that turned
out to be a very curious place indeed.

00:04:05.145 --> 00:04:10.107
Here Tom Chi, a Co-Founder of Google X
was unveiling the methods that

00:04:10.107 --> 00:04:15.071
Google X's engineers had used to
develop breakthrough technologies

00:04:15.071 --> 00:04:18.975
that put the Internet
on floating balloons.

00:04:18.975 --> 00:04:24.985
I was very excited and I was especially
enthusiastic, because the one thing our

00:04:24.985 --> 00:04:31.369
team hadn't figured out how to do was to
solve a bottleneck and engineering talent.

00:04:31.369 --> 00:04:34.160
Basically, how did we get
more technical skills and

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.476
technical people to help bring our
customer insights into real opportunities,

00:04:38.476 --> 00:04:40.069
and tools for our customers.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.410
As Tom began to share,

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:48.010
he talked about how a lot of the world's
wealth runs through its largest companies,

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:53.340
but companies don't actually develop
all of the technology that they create.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.630
And whether they develop it or not, they
tend to own the patents for 20 more years.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.970
What I started to realize is we couldn't
just trust that the world's big companies

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.600
would have a handle on
the innovation that we really need

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.190
to solve the problems we want
to see solved in our lifetime.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.998
Instead, it was going to take a lot of
people working together really quickly in

00:05:14.998 --> 00:05:18.515
order to tackle problems on
the scale of climate change and

00:05:18.515 --> 00:05:21.409
we were going to need to
get started right away.

00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:25.489
As Tom shared the method,
I started to become astonished.

00:05:25.489 --> 00:05:31.041
Because what I imagined would be a highly
math intensive engineering specific

00:05:31.041 --> 00:05:36.342
discipline really boiled down to three
steps that Google access engineers

00:05:36.342 --> 00:05:42.250
use over and over, and over to reduce
uncertainty, and bring the reality.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.730
Bring the present in the future
into present reality.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.560
Step one, start with a skit.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.284
Step two,

00:05:49.284 --> 00:05:54.679
build something basic using pretty
accessible kindergarten level supplies.

00:05:54.679 --> 00:05:57.310
And step three, change as you go.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.010
Again and again,
they repeated this process.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:05.189
And in the room, we use post-its and
scissors to do the same thing.

00:06:05.189 --> 00:06:06.349
Start with a skit.

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:07.449
Build something basic.

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:08.919
Change as you go.

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:10.430
Start with a skit.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:11.520
Build something basic.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.380
Change as you go.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.494
The more we repeated this process,
the more it started to sink in.

00:06:18.495 --> 00:06:22.985
It wasn't just my team at Google that
had had everything we needed to move

00:06:22.985 --> 00:06:28.115
forward on building technology in
our corporate context, but actually

00:06:28.115 --> 00:06:33.535
the classrooms I had grown up in in
Kansas had everything we needed then too.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:36.330
I was infuriated.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:41.719
I'm not sure where got the message
that in order to create the future,

00:06:41.719 --> 00:06:45.349
I needed access to the best science and
technology labs and

00:06:45.349 --> 00:06:49.069
teachers who had 20 years of
experience in data science.

00:06:49.069 --> 00:06:53.764
But at this point, I started to realize
that if we all truly believe in this

00:06:53.764 --> 00:06:55.961
potential, a lot could change and

00:06:55.961 --> 00:06:59.929
a lot more people could be
involved in directing that change.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:06.508
The next stage in my career was
a three-year stint testing my hypothesis

00:07:06.508 --> 00:07:09.860
that this was a basic process
that anybody could learn.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:15.220
I taught people from 50 organizations
from the US Department of Labor

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.040
to Girl Scouts at a science and tech camp.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:23.450
I taught corporate, entrepreneurial and
nonprofit sector leaders from Norway

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:28.370
to Vietnam and including the American
Midwest and the biggest insight I

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:33.980
had from that journey was a workshop I
taught to undergrads in Central Iowa.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.079
The workshop began and everyone started,
and very quickly as I do.

00:07:38.079 --> 00:07:39.490
I said, let's going.

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:41.420
Time to build, right?

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:46.510
Yehey, and this young woman named Chelsea
looked up at me and said, I can't do it.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.589
I can't go forward and I said, why not?

00:07:51.589 --> 00:07:55.795
She looks at me and said,
you're asking me to create and

00:07:55.795 --> 00:07:58.849
no ones ever even told
me I can be creative

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.060
That's when I realized that there
are thousands of creators besides my

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.910
original self who don't even
realize that we can create.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.660
And in fact, we are creating all the time.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:18.670
But our own lack of awareness that,
that's what we're already up to and

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:23.106
it's absolutely us who can do it
holds us back from really making

00:08:23.106 --> 00:08:26.249
headway on the things
we care about the most.

00:08:26.249 --> 00:08:30.980
What I learned over the course of
this journey is that imagination and

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:35.726
invention aren't hyperspecific
technologies that no one can use.

00:08:35.726 --> 00:08:39.939
It's that actually,
it's more like imagination is a muscle.

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:47.670
It's a process that anybody can pickup
that you can exercise and develop.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:51.780
And by practicing in this method,
you can begin to make real progress

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:55.400
on the things you care about and
your community cares about the most and

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:00.120
you can empower your workforce and
your economy to do exactly the same.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.720
If we really live like
imagination as a muscle,

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.350
you all can make a huge difference,
even in the rest of this week.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:13.660
Maybe you would start by looking around
you for people with wild ideas who

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:18.170
really could just grab a piece of scrap
paper and start to draw what the wireframe

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:22.210
interface would look like rather than
applying to another accelerator.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:24.220
Or worse yet,
quitting if they don't get in.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:29.029
You could look around at the groups,
the communities and

00:09:29.029 --> 00:09:33.626
the workforces that you are a part of for
places where actually a lot more is

00:09:33.626 --> 00:09:37.060
possible than humans just
doing routine activities.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:42.610
And if you did, you might find out
that the unemployment graph flipped.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:46.091
Starts to look like
capacity to innovate and

00:09:46.091 --> 00:09:51.925
you could put your ear to the ground for
some innovators, either likely or

00:09:51.925 --> 00:09:59.510
unlikely who might not yet have been told
that, in fact, they too can be creative.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:04.700
Returning to your routine today, I invite
you to start this process by keeping

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:09.250
an open mind for maybe a project that you
haven't been able to move forward yet.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:13.315
Is there something that you can just
put on a rough sheet of paper and

00:10:13.315 --> 00:10:14.850
hand to a friend to test?

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:22.008
If you did, I think you might discover
that you are far more inventive,

00:10:22.008 --> 00:10:26.793
even very rapidly than
you ever dared to think.

00:10:26.793 --> 00:10:30.302
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:30.302 --> 00:10:33.100
[MUSIC]

